      "weight_fields_interpretation": "The study uses PISA 2015 science data (79,963 students aged 15) and constructs a dichotomous outcome based on science proficiency levels (Level 6 vs Levels 1a/1b). The analytic sample is restricted to 53 countries/economies due to missing/void values in 19 other countries/economies, and the dependent variable is derived from PISA science plausible values (PV1SCIE). The manuscript does not report whether PISA sampling weights or replicate weights were applied in the SVM training/evaluation; given the use of SVM and Python-based preprocessing (imputation, dummy coding, centering/scaling), weights are likely omitted or not discussed. For replication, analysts should use the appropriate PISA student sampling weights (and replicate weights if variance estimation is required) from the PISA 2015 database, but the paper’s results should be interpreted as potentially unweighted with respect to complex survey design."
