    "main_findings": [
      {
        "dataset_used": "PISA 2015 Science (15-year-olds), dichotomized Level 6 vs Levels 1a/1b",
        "target_variable": "Science performance group (high vs low)",
        "top_predictors": [
          "Educational aspiration",
          "Occupational expectations",
          "Epistemological beliefs",
          "Enjoyment of science",
          "Teacher unfairness"
        ],
        "performance_metrics": "SVM (with SVM-RFE feature selection) — ACC: 0.7315, AUC: 0.7017, F measure: 0.7315, SEN: 0.7727, Precision: 0.7072. Optimal feature set (30 features) — ACC: 0.8561, AUC: 0.8103, F measure: 0.7942, SEN: 0.8286, Precision: 0.8107.",
        "standardized_conclusion": "Using PISA 2015 Science the study leveraged educational aspiration, occupational expectations, epistemological beliefs, enjoyment of science, and teacher unfairness to predict students’ science performance group (high vs low), finding that the SVM achieved fair classification performance (ACC≈0.73; AUC≈0.70) and improved substantially when using the optimal 30-feature set (ACC≈0.86; AUC≈0.81). This indicates that a multidimensional set of school, classroom, and student contextual factors can reliably differentiate high- and low-performing science students, supporting the dynamic model of educational effectiveness for equity-oriented school improvement, though the results are predictive (not causal) and rely on PV1SCIE with nearest-neighbor mean imputation."
      }
    ],
    "outcome_summary": "The study uses PISA 2015 science data for 15-year-olds from 53 countries/economies (N=79,963) and defines a dichotomous outcome: high performers at science proficiency Level 6 (score > 633.33) versus low performers at Levels 1a/1b. Because PISA provides 10 plausible values, the authors randomly select PV1SCIE to represent each student’s science score, then train an SVM classifier with SVM-RFE to rank 127 contextual factors across student, classroom, and school levels. After preprocessing (discarding missing/invalid questionnaire data, nearest-neighbor mean imputation, dummy coding, and centering/scaling), the baseline SVM reaches ACC=0.7315 and AUC=0.7017, while the optimal 30-feature set improves performance to ACC=0.8561 and AUC=0.8103 (with SEN=0.8286 and Precision=0.8107). The paper does not report survey-weight usage and uses cross-sectional ML classification, so findings should be interpreted as predictive evidence for the dynamic model rather than causal effects.",
    "research_design_type": "predictive",
